BT /F2 22 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 765.19 Tm (Operating Systems) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 741.19 Tm (Chapter 4: Process Scheduling  |  Year 2 lecture handout) Tj ET
0.75 w 0.7 0.7 0.7 RG 56.70 733.19 m 538.58 733.19 l S
BT /F2 13 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 692.19 Tm (4.1 The scheduling problem) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 668.19 Tm (A computer with one processor can run only one process at a time. Every other ready) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 652.69 Tm (process waits in a queue. The scheduler is the part of the operating system that) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 637.19 Tm (decides which process runs next and for how long. Good scheduling keeps the processor) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 621.69 Tm (busy, keeps response time short, and treats every process fairly.) Tj ET
BT /F2 13 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 584.19 Tm (4.2 When the scheduler runs) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 560.19 Tm (The scheduler is called when a process moves from running to waiting, when a process) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 544.69 Tm (moves from running to ready, when a process moves from waiting to ready, and when a) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 529.19 Tm (process terminates. The first and the last of these are non preemptive: the running) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 513.69 Tm (process gives up the processor on its own. The other two are preemptive: the operating) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 498.19 Tm (system takes the processor away.) Tj ET
BT /F2 13 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 460.69 Tm (4.3 Scheduling criteria) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 436.69 Tm (Throughput is the number of processes completed per unit of time. Turnaround time is) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 421.19 Tm (the time from submission to completion. Waiting time is the total time spent in the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 405.69 Tm (ready queue. Response time is the time from submission to the first response. No) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 390.19 Tm (algorithm optimises all four at once, so every scheduler is a compromise.) Tj ET
endstream
