BT /F2 13 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 760.19 Tm (4.6 Round robin) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 736.19 Tm (Round robin gives every ready process a turn in a fixed time slice, called a quantum.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 720.69 Tm (When the slice ends the process goes to the back of the ready queue and the next) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 705.19 Tm (process runs. The size of the slice decides how the system feels.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 681.69 Tm (A time slice that is too short turns the processor into a switchboard: too much time) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 666.19 Tm (goes to saving and restoring context, and too little to useful work. A time slice that) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 650.69 Tm (is too long makes round robin behave like first come first served, and a student) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 635.19 Tm (clicking a button waits behind a batch job. A slice of about ten to one hundred) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 619.69 Tm (milliseconds is the usual compromise.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 596.19 Tm (Worked example. Five processes arrive at the same time with bursts of 8, 4, 2, 1 and 1) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 580.69 Tm (milliseconds. With a quantum of 2 milliseconds the first process finishes in its fourth) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 565.19 Tm (turn, so response time stays short for the four short jobs. With a quantum of 8) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56.70 549.69 Tm (milliseconds the short jobs wait for the whole first burst.) Tj ET
endstream
